   "source": "run manifests under results/raw/"
  }
 ],
 "limitations": [
  "No perception claims. All metrics are objective timings in a browser. Nothing here measures how fast an interface feels to a person. Any perceived-speed statement needs a human study, which is out of scope and would need an owner-approved protocol.",
  "One mock interface. Results hold for one synthetic page with fixed layout and content. Real applications differ in DOM size, framework, caching and network behaviour.",
  "Synthetic latency. Delays come from a seeded mock transport, not from a network. Bandwidth, connection setup, server variance and tail latency are not modelled beyond a uniform jitter of plus or minus 10%.",
  "One machine and one engine. Chromium on an Apple M1 Pro laptop. Other engines (WebKit, Gecko), other devices and mobile hardware are not covered.",
  "Instrumentation limits. Frame times are estimated in-page; Event Timing hides durations under 16 ms and rounds to 8 ms; headless rendering may not match a physical display.",
  "Scripted input. Interactions and prefetch intent signals are scripted with fixed timing; real pointer behaviour is not modelled.",
  "Prefetch results are partly mechanical. The benefit is bounded by the scripted lead time; the experiment measures whether the implementation and instrumentation recover it, and its costs, not how often real users hover before clicking.",
  "Simulation status. All results are simulations within the stated assumptions; they are not claims about any real or proprietary product.",
  "Frame-scale effects depend on the paint-time method. EXP-002 found SKELETON minus BLANK of -16.7 ms at L = 50 ms (equivalence not shown); under the alternative paint methods the estimate was -15.3 ms (raf1) and -9.1 ms (Element Timing), both equivalent within 17 ms (EXP-004 S1). Differences of about one frame are therefore method-dependent and are not interpreted as strategy effects.",
  "Sign rule on null contrasts. The EXP-004 robustness rule compares signs of point estimates. For the write-path failure strata the main estimates are within about 3 ms of zero and not significant, so their sign changes across levels carry no information about direction.",
  "Test reading. The protocol names a bootstrap interval, a Wilcoxon test and a Holm correction without stating which one decides H0. DEC-011 fixed the reading before data (Holm over Wilcoxon p-values); one contrast (EXP-002 SKELETON, L = 300 ms) differs between the two readings.",
  "TTFF sensitivity not computed. The alternative paint method is recorded only for the usable-content frame, so the TTFF part of sensitivity S1 is missing (DEC-012).",
  "Reduced sensitivity coverage. S2 to S4 use 10 seeds, L in {300, 1000} ms and p in {0, 0.3}; cells outside that set have only the S1 check.",
  "Run conditions. One browser version (Chrome 158.0.8127.38), runs under nice -n 10, load average 1.4 to 2.4 at run starts with an editor and system services running. The exclusive window reduced, but did not remove, background activity.",
  "Margin smaller than a measured frame. The 17 ms equivalence margin was justified as one 60 Hz frame, but the headless frame interval estimated from the second-frame gap was 18.0 to 18.9 ms in every cell (exploratory X2). A one-frame shift under the main method cannot pass the TOST; this bears on the 50 ms SKELETON result, which is reported as not robust.",
  "rAF1 is a frame-start time. The rAF1 alternative of S1 precedes the reference delay in most trials (72 to 79% of BLANK read trials at L of 100 ms and more), so it is a lower, optimistic estimate, not a paint time. Whether it belongs in S1 is part of DEC-011 (pending owner review); under the narrow reading (Element Timing only) 9 write contrasts have no sensitivity level at all.",
  "Literal contradiction criterion. The protocol's criterion for contradicting the main interpretation is literally met for four contrasts (the 50 ms SKELETON equivalence and three failure-stratum sign changes). The manuscript reports the interpretation as qualified; that judgement is post hoc.",
  "Headed-mode confound. In S4 the frame cadence after input differed between arms (second-frame gap 8.6 to 9.0 ms for OPTIMISTIC against 16.45 to 16.75 ms for BLANK on the write path), and S4 ran one to three hours after the main runs. The headed shift of the optimistic benefit may reflect display behaviour or time drift rather than headed rendering as such. The refresh rate was not recorded.",
  "External sources. Every cited source was first opened as a model-written page summary (M0). In M6 (2026-11-25) the full text of the nine free web sources was read and each cited claim was confirmed against the cited section; Mejtoft et al. (2018) has no free full text and stays metadata only, used for no finding. The literature search itself remains a short, non-systematic scoping search.",
  "No new measurement in M4. The machine was shared with other agents during M4, so sensitivity and confounder work used committed traces only (results/exploratory/M4-REANALYSIS/). Questions that need new runs (display refresh rate, first-feedback paint times, frame-loop state at short delays) remain open."
 ],
